The `test_msgq_full` test case fails because the user-mode thread `put_full_entry` attempts to access global message queue (`msgq1`) and data (`data`) variables without having the necessary permissions. In Zephyr's user mode, threads require explicit access grants to kernel objects and global memory regions that are not automatically accessible.

The `msgq_api_setup` function, which is executed before the test suite, is responsible for granting these permissions. However, it currently only grants access to a subset of the global variables used by the tests. `msgq1`, `tdata1`, `tdata2`, `tstack1`, `tstack2`, `data`, and `msg3` are all global variables or kernel objects that are accessed by user-mode threads in `test_msgq_contexts.c` but are not included in the `k_thread_access_grant` call.

The fix involves extending the `k_thread_access_grant` call in `msgq_api_setup` to include all necessary global variables and kernel objects that user-mode threads will interact with.

